@iluuu1994 Thanks! I can confirm that the Revive Test Suite fails to run because of:

```
PHP Notice:  Only variables should be passed by reference in /home/matteo/OSS/php-src/r.php on line 8
```

vs:

```
Fatal error: Uncaught Error: A::__construct(): Argument #1 ($o) could not be passed by reference in /home/matteo/OSS/php-src/r.php:8
```

which I'm totally OK with, but will probably need to be listed in UPGRADING (or RFC?).

Simple enough to replicate:
```
<?php

class A {
   public function __construct(&$o) {
   }
}

var_dump(new A(new \StdClass()));
```

@mbeccati After thinking about this some more, and discussing with @bwoebi, I think the second case can be considered a fix. As mentioned, PHP already errors when things that can never be references are passed to by-ref parameters. For example:

https://3v4l.org/Irv4C

```php
namespace Foo;

function takesRef(&$ref) {}

takesRef(count([])); // This will warn
takesRef(\count([])); // This will throw
```

The first _may_ be a locally-shadowing `count` function that returns by-ref, which makes PHP only emit a notice. The second is a known call to the global `count` function, so PHP will throw an exception. I won't try to argue for _why_ that would be sensible behavior (it's not). I'd support the consolidation of behavior (either promoting or relaxing both errors, I don't particularly care which way around). In either case, special-handling `new` here probably doesn't make sense. I'll make sure to add a note in the UPRADING file.

@mbeccati What would need a RFC would be _reversing_ the behaviour. This is a pure bugfix.
Allowing new or any other arbitrary expression to be passed to args expecting by ref.
I would welcome that change though.

